---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Agile, Not Fragile"
  description="A practical view of Agile delivery: context-dependent, operationally accountable and never a substitute for sound planning."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Delivery Perspective</p>
        <h1>Agile, <span>not fragile.</span></h1>
        <p class="summary">
          Agile is a useful way to deliver in uncertain, evolving environments.
          It is not a one-size-fits-all method, a shortcut around planning, or
          a promise that delivery will automatically cost less.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="principles">
        <p class="eyebrow">The position</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Choose the delivery model to fit the work.</li>
          <li>Keep outcomes, risk and accountability visible.</li>
          <li>Treat operations as an equal delivery partner.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="container content-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why It Matters</p>
        <h2>Good delivery is deliberate, not ideological.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Teams need a way to learn, adjust and deliver value without losing
          control of reliability, security, cost or stakeholder confidence.
          Agile can provide that rhythm when the work has uncertainty, requires
          regular feedback or benefits from incremental delivery.
        </p>
        <p>
          But the framework is not the outcome. The important question is
          whether the approach helps the team make better decisions and deliver
          a service that can be safely supported after release.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Fit the Method to the Work</p>
        <h2>When Agile fits — and when it does not.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="comparison">
        <article>
          <p class="label">Agile is a strong fit when</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Requirements are likely to evolve through user or operational feedback.</li>
            <li>The work can be delivered and validated in meaningful increments.</li>
            <li>Teams can collaborate regularly and make timely decisions.</li>
            <li>Learning early reduces the risk of building the wrong thing.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="label">A planned or hybrid approach is better when</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Scope, dependencies and acceptance criteria are well understood.</li>
            <li>There are fixed regulatory, contractual or implementation windows.</li>
            <li>Change carries high service, safety or operational risk.</li>
            <li>Up-front design and controlled sequencing reduce uncertainty.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Waterfall, Agile and hybrid delivery are tools. The right choice depends
        on the risk, certainty, dependencies and operating environment — not on
        what is fashionable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="container content-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The Cost Reality</p>
        <h2>Agile is not automatically cheaper than Waterfall.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Agile changes the cost profile; it does not remove cost. Frequent
          planning, refinement, demonstrations, testing, stakeholder engagement
          and continuous prioritisation all need time from capable people.
        </p>
        <p>
          It may reduce expensive late rework when learning is genuinely useful.
          It can also cost more when scope remains open without disciplined
          prioritisation, when teams attend ceremonies without making decisions,
          or when operational work is left outside the delivery plan.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Dev and Ops</p>
        <h2>Avoid capital DEV and lowercase ops.</h2>
        <p>Software is not complete when code is merged. It is complete when it can be run, supported, secured and recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="delivery-flow">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Discover and design</h3><p>Define the user outcome, service impact, constraints and operational measures of success.</p></article>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Build and test</h3><p>Develop with security, observability, failure modes and maintainability considered from the start.</p></article>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Release and operate</h3><p>Deploy with runbooks, monitoring, ownership and support readiness in place.</p></article>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Learn and improve</h3><p>Use real operational feedback to improve the product, platform and delivery approach.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        This is not an argument for collapsing every role into one person. It is
        an argument for shared accountability: development and operations should
        have equal voice in the decisions that affect the service.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="container outcome">
      <p class="eyebrow">Practical Experience</p>
      <h2>Use the discipline, not just the vocabulary.</h2>
      <p>
        My approach is to combine iterative delivery with the right level of
        planning, technical governance, documentation and operational
        involvement. The goal is dependable change: delivered at a sensible
        pace, understood by the people who run it, and measured against the
        outcome it was intended to achieve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero { padding: 7rem 0 4rem; } .hero-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(280px, .6fr); gap: 4rem; align-items: start; }
  h1 { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; font-size: clamp(3.6rem, 8vw, 6.5rem); line-height: .95; letter-spacing: -.06em; } h1 span { color: var(--primary); }
  h2 { margin: 0; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem); line-height: 1.05; } .summary, .prose p, .heading > p:last-child, .outcome > p:last-child { max-width: 760px; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.12rem; }
  .principles, .comparison article, .delivery-flow article { padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--surface); } .principles ul, .comparison ul { margin: 1rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; } .principles li, .comparison li { margin: .6rem 0; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .content-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: .8fr 1.2fr; gap: 5rem; } .comparison { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; } .label { margin: 0; color: var(--primary); font-weight: 800; }
  .note { max-width: 900px; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--primary); color: var(--text-muted); }
  .delivery-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr); gap: .8rem; align-items: stretch; } .delivery-flow span { color: var(--primary); font-weight: 800; } .delivery-flow p { color: var(--text-muted); } .arrow { display: grid; place-items: center; color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: 800; } .outcome { max-width: 900px; }
  @media (max-width: 850px) { .hero-grid, .content-grid, .comparison, .delivery-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .arrow { transform: rotate(90deg); min-height: 1.75rem; } }
</style>
